A||||15||four50four||engine swap vs. turbo 3.2?||||1
Z||000000||four50four||11-11-1999||07:23 PM||||when i get my fiero, i plan on giving it some serious power. well i was roughly tallying up the price of doing a perfromance rebuild w/turbo, and it came out pretty high. would it be cheaper to just do an engine swap? i want to keep it a v-6, or even a quad 4, but leaning towards a 3.4 or 3.4 tdc. which one would you guys reccommend? heres a link...  <A HREF="http://members.xoom.com/fierov6/OSG/mod.3400-TDC-V6.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.xoom.com/fierov6/OSG/mod.3400-TDC-V6.html</A> <BR>is it possible to find one of these engines for $800? are there any problems associated with them, ex overheating, blowing up? any idea on how much they weigh? how hard will this swap be? between be and my dad we are pretty handy with vehicles, and normal engine swaps (trucks, minivans) aren't very hard? an finally, will doing an engine swap effect insurance? thanks in advance. also, should i just forget this and get a normal push-rod 3.4, and do it up to get same power output. which would be cheaper. the 3.4 TDC would definatly be my first choice, but if the normal 3.4 is considerablly cheaper....<p>[This message has been edited by four50four (edited 11-11-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||four50four||11-11-1999||08:37 PM||||american custom engineering has a kit for $1800. i imagine if i could find a good engine out of a wrecked car, and install it for less than a performance rebuild and turbo, as for a that i would be expecting to spend AT LEAST $3500. any thoughts?||||reg||1
Z||000002||88 Fiero||11-11-1999||09:28 PM||||when you said ACE or American Engineering has a kit for $1,800 is it for the Quad 4 kit or the 3.4...You won't need the kit for the 3.4 block..because everything hooks up with the 2.8 V6 engine...the only thing you need to is to relocate the starter...Now for the Quad 4 ...I don't know...it seems like they always have a head gasket problems...I don't know about that...but why spend money on a Quad4, if you prefer to swap a different engine...just do a V8 swap and you will be happy with the performance...The 3.4 will look stock and it won't affect your insurance policy unless you will report to your agent that you just finish installing a 3.4 block or any kind of engine to your Fiero....||||reg||1
Z||000003||four50four||11-11-1999||09:32 PM||||they have a $1800 kit for both the quad 4 and the 3.4 TDC, not the 3.4 camaro pushrod engine. sure i like v-8s, but i would rather have a v-6, due to fuel economy and weight.||||reg||1
Z||000004||88 Fiero||11-11-1999||09:35 PM||||If that's the case go with the 3.4 TDC instead of the Quad4.....||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tim||11-11-1999||10:59 PM||||Cam belt and idler are the weak link with TDC.  Also the HP comes at high RPM.  I think I saw 210 HP @ 7200 RPM.  No low-end torque.  Is pretty and trick.  If current engine is good, then use bolt-on products that can be transferred to new engine when ready.  If current engine is bad then buy 3.4 crate from GM, a good solid base to build on.<p>[This message has been edited by Tim (edited 11-12-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||11-15-1999||08:13 AM||||The camaro 3.4L engine puts out about 200 hp and bolts up with a few modifications.  The starter needs to be moved to the other side of the engine and a stud needs to be welded to the A/C bracket to give a place for the shock strut to mount to.  Also the flywheel needs to be either from the camaro or from an 88GT.<BR>Ed Parks at the Fiero Factory ((256) 420-5391  or Fierofactory@juno.com) sells modified low mileage (meaning less than 50000 miles) 3.4L engines for around $1400 or so.  He also can get you a Grooms remanufactured motor and modify it for you to fit.<P>I'd suggest porting the exhaust manifolds during the swap unless you want to spend $500+ for a set of headers.||||reg||1
Z||000007||88GT||11-15-1999||12:43 PM||||What about going with a Turbocharged 3800 series II?  I know there a bit more money but they seem to put out good power in a good package. <P>I'm still new at the engine swap game so forgive me if I might be wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000008||88 Fiero||11-15-1999||01:12 PM||||Carolles...the 3.4 Camaro engine puts out 165HP with 200lbs. of Torque....althougha there are alot of performance parts available for this engine... I myself will install one with a turbo StageII in it... I also found out the the 1999 Grand Am GT comes with a 3.4 block that puts out 175HP and 205 or 210lbs. of Torque...But it's an aluminum engine...I don't know whether the block can be use with our 2.8 or will it work with our 2.8. Does anybody know????????||||reg||1
Z||000009||mwbackus||11-15-1999||01:55 PM||||The stock Camaro 3.4 was only rated at 160hp , same as the S-10 HT3.4 package.  The "HT" stands for high torque.  This motor is rated as 160hp @ 5,000 RPM but torque is 195 Ft. Lbs. @ 2700 RPM vs. the Camaro's 200 lbs/ft at 3600 rpm.<BR>This low rpm torque is due to the 12363220 camshaft which has about the same specs as the GM cam that seems to be quite popular for 3.2 rebuilds.  Of course the rest of the valvetrain is built to match this cam (ie. high pressure springs, better pushrods, etc.) What this means to us gearheads is that a whole lot of modification can be employed to increase horsepower before the peak torque curve is shifted into a less street usable area. It should be noted that both engines are available as "New" shortblocks, the following prices are from Pace GM Performance Parts:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.paceparts.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.paceparts.com/</A>  <BR> <BR>12363230  HT3.4 (New)        $1,669.50<P>12537104  Camaro 3.4 (New)   $1,516.80<P><BR>Mike<BR>(Fiero info located in my user profile)||||reg||1
Z||000010||88GT||11-16-1999||12:33 AM||||Will a stock 5 speed with a performance cluth handle a 3800 Series II or is that just too much.<P>Also what kind of HP gains can one expect from a turbo on a 2.6 V6?||||reg||5
Z||000011||turbo86se||11-16-1999||11:51 PM||||88GT: I've got a mint black 86SE with a Miller Woods turbo. 50,000 miles, auto trans. I'd say I'm running about 200-220 horsepower. Only slight modifications, mainly to exhaust:<BR>*Ported and polished manifolds<BR>*Jet Hot coated manifolds<BR>*Removed cat. convertor<BR>*K&N air filter<BR>*Ultimate Motor Mount <P>I've had a couple conversations with Jim Sutter, president of IRM, he informed me this is about as much as I can do while keeping the engine reliable. The turbo is made to run on a stock V6. I have been thinking of swaping the 2.8 with a 3.4, I've seen it on a convertable Fiero from Alabama, and it did look completely stock, sure didn't sound like it! <P>If you live near Myrtle Beach, let me know, I'll let you experience a turbo-charged Fiero first hand!||||reg||1
Z||000012||88GT||11-17-1999||12:03 AM||||TURBO86SE<P>I'm trying to decide whether to do an engine swap or add a turbo charger.  Unfortunately, money is an object and the engine swaps just seem sooooo expensive.  I would love to put in a 3800SC Series II.||||reg||1
Z||000013||88GT||11-17-1999||12:31 AM||||I guess I was wrong.  I thought a Turbo upgrade would be reasonable.  I saw one for like 3,000.  I would rather do an engine swap and get even more power and pay that much for a Turbo Charger!||||reg||11
Z||000014||WKDFIRO||11-17-1999||04:25 AM||||88 GT,<P>Grand Am 3.4 (as well as the Trans Port 3.4) are different. The oil pan is completely different configuration among other things...<P>As far as the Getrag/Muncie 5 speed, we had a stock tranny hooked up with a mulitdisk clutch on a 91 corvette engine making 330 hp on the dyno. Drove all the way to Michigan from Southern California. I think even a supercharged 3800 can be handled with that.||||reg||1
Z||000015||fastonpumpgas||11-19-1999||12:16 AM||||I am doing the 3.2 conversion, and beleive me there is nothing cheap about gettin power out of a fiero.  I figure I will be right about 1500 dollars in part all said and done, with around 175HP .  I am not sure it is worth and, and as far as gas prices I will prolly have to run 92 octane all the time, but stil alot cheaper than a 3.4 or a 3800 or small block switch.   I guess the more you pay the power you get !!!I would say save up and get what you really want.  but the 3.4 and the 3800 are more expensive than you think<BR>||||reg||1
